Add Express middleware to log requests and responses. This applies across all routes in one place without editing each handler.

Create a logging middleware that:
- Logs the incoming request (method, path, `req.user.id` for the authenticated user)
- Captures the response status code and duration
- Runs before your routes, in the app setup (presumably in app.js)

Example shape:
```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    const duration = Date.now() - start;
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} ${res.statusCode} ${duration}ms user=${req.user?.id}`);
  });
  next();
});
```

This logs after the response is sent. If you also want to log errors, add error handling middleware after your routes that logs exceptions before the response.

Alternatively, if you want to log every database operation, you could add logging inside `db.write()` and `db.query()` to see the actual SQL and query time—that's orthogonal to request logging and useful for spotting slow queries.

